
==============================================================================
A. DATA INTEGRITY / QUANTISATION  (ground rule 2)
==============================================================================
fanON_100k_0    2500000 @   100 kSa/s  25.0 s  codes= 5  LSB= 336.0 uV  rms= 157.6 uV  DC= 11.866 mV  zeros=0
fanON_100k_1    2500000 @   100 kSa/s  25.0 s  codes= 5  LSB= 336.0 uV  rms= 153.5 uV  DC= 11.847 mV  zeros=0
fanOFF_100k      600000 @   100 kSa/s   6.0 s  codes= 5  LSB= 336.0 uV  rms= 173.6 uV  DC= 12.996 mV  zeros=0
fanON_50k_a     3000000 @    50 kSa/s  60.0 s  codes= 4  LSB= 336.0 uV  rms= 118.3 uV  DC= 11.760 mV  zeros=0
fanON_50k_b     3000000 @    50 kSa/s  60.0 s  codes= 4  LSB= 336.0 uV  rms= 108.1 uV  DC= 11.778 mV  zeros=0
fanOFF_50k      2991808 @    50 kSa/s  59.8 s  codes= 4  LSB= 336.0 uV  rms= 158.4 uV  DC= 13.250 mV  zeros=0

  Only 4-5 distinct ADC codes in every record (LSB 336 uV), as FINDINGS 1.1
  predicts. Detection depends entirely on FFT processing gain.
  Fan-ON records sit ~1.2-1.5 mV lower in DC and ~1.5-2 dB lower in
  broadband floor than the fan-OFF references, which came from a different
  session. ABSOLUTE level is therefore not comparable between conditions;
  only spectral SHAPE is. Cepstrum/HPS use shape only.

==============================================================================
B. POSITIVE CONTROLS  (does the method work at all, on real data?)
==============================================================================

B1. REAL known comb: FMCW Config-A ramp feedthrough, truth = 50.000 Hz
    cepstrum  -> 50.01 Hz  (313 sigma over its own measured floor)
    HPS(K=5)  -> 150.14 Hz  (HPS also locks onto 3x = 150 Hz, the standard
                 HPS harmonic ambiguity; the cepstrum does not suffer it)

B2. REAL known comb #2: 60 Hz mains in the empty-room baseline (FINDINGS 3)
    cepstrum  -> 60.41 Hz  (6.7 sigma)   truth 60.00 Hz
    HPS(K=5)  -> 60.01 Hz
    quefrency bin = 0.338 ms wide = +-1.19 Hz at a 60 Hz spacing, so 59.4 and
    60.0 Hz are the same cepstral bin. The mains cepstral peak is weak (only
    h=1,3,5 stand out, so the comb is far from uniform) but HPS nails it.

B3. KNOWN MOVING PERSON via consecutive-chirp MTI (FINDINGS 5.5: +6.7 dB,
    12 sigma at 1.5 m). Not my method -- this checks that the person really
    is in the file I loaded and that my sigma machinery reproduces it.
    MEASURED split-half floor (same condition, two halves) = 0.55 dB
    R= 1.0 m  moving  -34.44  static  -39.60  diff +5.16 dB  (  9.4 sigma)
    R= 1.5 m  moving  -28.93  static  -35.79  diff +6.85 dB  ( 12.4 sigma)
    R= 2.5 m  moving  -29.43  static  -36.10  diff +6.67 dB  ( 12.1 sigma)
    R= 4.0 m  moving  -37.42  static  -39.30  diff +1.87 dB  (  3.4 sigma)

B4. KNOWN MOVING PERSON via CEPSTRUM/HPS (the assigned method itself).
    12 vs 8 blocks of 0.4 s, df=12.2 Hz, 308 quefrency bins searched
    TEST  moving vs static  : largest |sigma| = 3.88 at spacing 52.4 Hz
    NULL  static 1st vs 2nd half (4v4): largest |sigma| = 5.41
    NULL  moving 1st vs 2nd half (6v6): largest |sigma| = 3.80
    -> cepstrum/HPS DOES NOT DETECT the walking person.
    Expected: a walking human is not a frequency-periodic comb at any
    spacing this data resolves (df=12 Hz here; gait cadence is ~2 Hz).

B5. SENSITIVITY CALIBRATION. A synthetic blade comb (spacing 73.0 Hz --
    deliberately NOT a multiple of 60 -- lines from 73 Hz to 4.0 kHz, random
    phase) is added to the FIRST half of the real empty-room record; the
    SECOND half stays clean and acts as the reference. Identical pipeline.
    NOTE a comb of spacing D also puts cepstral rahmonics at q = n/D, i.e.
    apparent spacings D/2, D/3, ... The pass criterion below is the sigma AT
    the true quefrency 1/73 s, not the location of the global maximum
    (which often lands on a rahmonic).
    comb  -46 dB re IF rms (54 lines) -> global max   45.15 Hz @   +5.4 sig                 ;  sigma AT 73.0 Hz =   -1.2
    comb  -40 dB re IF rms (54 lines) -> global max   45.15 Hz @   +5.0 sig                 ;  sigma AT 73.0 Hz =   -1.7
    comb  -34 dB re IF rms (54 lines) -> global max   45.15 Hz @   +4.8 sig                 ;  sigma AT 73.0 Hz =   -2.3
    comb  -30 dB re IF rms (54 lines) -> global max   45.15 Hz @   +4.7 sig                 ;  sigma AT 73.0 Hz =   -0.7
    comb  -28 dB re IF rms (54 lines) -> global max   45.15 Hz @   +4.5 sig                 ;  sigma AT 73.0 Hz =   +0.7
    comb  -26 dB re IF rms (54 lines) -> global max   12.16 Hz @   +5.1 sig (73/n rahmonic) ;  sigma AT 73.0 Hz =   +2.3
    comb  -24 dB re IF rms (54 lines) -> global max   12.16 Hz @   +8.8 sig (73/n rahmonic) ;  sigma AT 73.0 Hz =   +4.8
    comb  -22 dB re IF rms (54 lines) -> global max   12.14 Hz @  +12.6 sig (73/n rahmonic) ;  sigma AT 73.0 Hz =   +6.9
    comb  -20 dB re IF rms (54 lines) -> global max   12.16 Hz @  +16.2 sig (73/n rahmonic) ;  sigma AT 73.0 Hz =  +10.0
    comb  -18 dB re IF rms (54 lines) -> global max   12.14 Hz @  +16.8 sig (73/n rahmonic) ;  sigma AT 73.0 Hz =  +12.1
    -> the detector recovers the correct spacing above 5 sigma once the comb
       carries >= -22 dB relative to the wideband IF rms.

==============================================================================
C. FAN-ON vs FAN-OFF, cepstrum, matched 50 kSa/s / 60 s, EQUAL block counts
==============================================================================
  band 40-6000 Hz (0.25-37.3 m/s), df=3.052 Hz, 1953 bins, 8 blocks x 7.5 s
  707 quefrency bins searched -> comb spacings 8.3-667 Hz.
  A household fan is 900-1600 rpm with 3-5 blades: blade-pass 45-133 Hz,
  rotation 15-27 Hz. Both lie inside the searched span.

  TEST (fan present vs absent):
  fan ON run a  vs  fan OFF          max|sigma| =  4.42 at spacing   82.78 Hz
  fan ON run b  vs  fan OFF          max|sigma| =  4.25 at spacing    9.28 Hz

  NULL CONTROLS (same condition on both sides -- these MEASURE the real
  false-alarm floor of the whole detector, session drift included):
  fan ON a      vs  fan ON b         max|sigma| =  4.29 at spacing   11.94 Hz
  fan OFF 1st half vs 2nd half       max|sigma| =  5.31 at spacing   45.15 Hz
  fan ON a 1st half vs 2nd half      max|sigma| =  7.83

  pooled fan ON (16 blocks) vs fan OFF (8 blocks): max|sigma| = 4.26 at 82.78 Hz
  top 8 quefrency bins of the pooled test:
     q (ms)  spacing Hz     d_ceps        SE    sigma  60Hz-mult?
     12.080       82.78    +26.622     6.252    +4.26  -
     95.133       10.51    +13.319     3.711    +3.59  -
      8.557      116.86    +28.496     8.286    +3.44  -
    107.717        9.28     +8.893     2.638    +3.37  -
     45.469       21.99    +20.071     6.161    +3.26  -
     49.999       20.00    +31.594     9.756    +3.24  -
    101.173        9.88    -10.095     3.127    -3.23  -
     63.086       15.85    -18.328     5.754    -3.19  -

  Theory says |z|>3 should occur 1.9 times in 707 bins by chance.
  MEASURED null controls put the real floor at max|sigma| = 4.29 / 5.31 / 7.83
  Repeatability r of the ON-minus-OFF cepstral difference across the two
  independent fan-ON runs: +0.540. Same statistic with two halves of the
  fan-OFF record standing in for 'fan ON': +0.454 -- so that much correlation
  comes from the SHARED reference alone, not from the fan.

  RESTRICTED SEARCH. Constraining the hypothesis to physically plausible
  blade-pass rates (40-140 Hz: 3-5 blades at 800-1700 rpm) shrinks the
  search space and so lowers the bar a real detection has to clear:
  107 quefrency bins; |z|>3 expected 0.29 times by chance.
    fan ON a vs OFF    max|sigma| =  4.42 at  82.78 Hz blade-pass (1656 rpm on 3 blades, 993 on 5)
    fan ON b vs OFF    max|sigma| =  3.65 at 116.86 Hz blade-pass (2337 rpm on 3 blades, 1402 on 5)
    pooled ON vs OFF   max|sigma| =  4.26 at  82.78 Hz blade-pass (1656 rpm on 3 blades, 993 on 5)
    NULL ON a vs ON b  max|sigma| =  2.78 at  65.50 Hz blade-pass (1310 rpm on 3 blades, 786 on 5)

  CANDIDATE CONSISTENCY. A real blade comb must appear at the SAME spacing
  in both independent fan-ON runs. Evaluating every test at the quefrency
  picked by the pooled test (82.78 Hz spacing):
    fan ON a vs OFF    sigma at 82.78 Hz =  +4.42
    fan ON b vs OFF    sigma at 82.78 Hz =  +2.21
    NULL ON a vs ON b  sigma at 82.78 Hz =  +1.89
    run a picked this bin; run b gives +2.21 sigma there. A blade comb
    cannot be present in one 60 s fan-ON record and absent in the next.

  60 Hz RAHMONIC FAMILY (ground rule 1). The prominent cepstral peaks in
  BOTH conditions sit at q = n/60 s -- the mains comb. Difference there:
    q= 16.67 ms (n=1, spacing 60.0 Hz): ON-OFF  -0.90 sigma  |  NULL  +0.32
    q= 33.33 ms (n=2, spacing 30.0 Hz): ON-OFF  +1.25 sigma  |  NULL  -0.68
    q= 50.00 ms (n=3, spacing 20.0 Hz): ON-OFF  +3.24 sigma  |  NULL  +0.26
    q= 66.67 ms (n=4, spacing 15.0 Hz): ON-OFF  -0.86 sigma  |  NULL  +0.69
    q= 83.33 ms (n=5, spacing 12.0 Hz): ON-OFF  +3.03 sigma  |  NULL  -1.52
    q=100.00 ms (n=6, spacing 10.0 Hz): ON-OFF  -0.09 sigma  |  NULL  -0.70
    q=116.67 ms (n=7, spacing 8.6 Hz): ON-OFF  -0.44 sigma  |  NULL  +0.76
  The mains rahmonics do not grow with the fan, so the fan is not even
  detectable as an ELECTRICAL load on this receiver, let alone as a target.

