==============================================================================
E. MAINS COMB: does switching the fan on raise it? (ground rule 1)
==============================================================================
  fanON_50k_a  dB over median at 60*h (h=1,2,3,4,5,8,16,32,48):   21.7    8.1   16.2    6.9   10.1    3.2    2.3    1.0    4.6
  fanON_50k_b  dB over median at 60*h (h=1,2,3,4,5,8,16,32,48):   21.4    8.8   16.0    6.8   10.2    2.8    2.0    1.5    4.2
  fanOFF_50k   dB over median at 60*h (h=1,2,3,4,5,8,16,32,48):   23.7    8.0   17.4    6.5   11.6    2.5    1.6    1.6    3.4

  The mains comb is NOT stronger with the fan on -- the fundamental is
  ~2 dB WEAKER, tracking the same overall floor offset seen in A. So no
  candidate blade line here can be explained away as extra mains pickup,
  and equally no mains growth is masquerading as a fan detection.
  Computed false-tag rate of a '+-2 Hz of a 60 Hz multiple' rule above
  3 kHz: 6.67% of random frequencies. High-order mains tags are therefore
  treated as uninformative and are not used as evidence either way.

==============================================================================
F. CROSS-CHECK at 100 kSa/s (25 s x2 fan-ON vs 6 s fan-OFF)
==============================================================================
  Ground rule 5 in force. Blocks are forced to EQUAL DURATION (2.0 s) on
  both sides, so each block carries the same number of Welch averages and
  the two conditions have the same per-block scatter. What cannot be equal
  is the NUMBER of blocks: 12 for each 25 s fan-ON record, only 3 for the
  6 s fan-OFF reference. The 1/n1+1/n2 term carries that, but a variance
  estimated from 3 blocks (2 dof) is itself very noisy, so sigma here is
  unstable. The matched-n null below MEASURES how much that inflates it.
  fan ON 0 vs fan OFF            max|sigma| =  9.06 at spacing   12.32 Hz
  fan ON 1 vs fan OFF            max|sigma| =  8.16 at spacing   12.32 Hz
  fan ON 0 vs fan ON 1           max|sigma| =  3.51 at spacing   17.44 Hz  <- NULL, 12v12
  fan ON 0 early vs fan ON 0 late max|sigma| =  7.53  <- NULL, 3v3 same record
  fan ON 0 (12) vs fan ON 1 (3)  max|sigma| =  5.85  <- NULL, 12v3, same block counts as the test
  The 12-vs-3 SAME-CONDITION null reaches 5.9 sigma, i.e. the whole
  apparent significance of the 100 kSa/s test is an artefact of estimating
  a variance from 3 reference blocks. This dataset cannot decide anything;
  the duration-matched 50 kSa/s comparison in section C is the real test.
  CROSS-RATE CHECK. The 100 kSa/s candidate spacing is 12.32 Hz. In the
  duration-matched 50 kSa/s test (section C) that same spacing gives:
    fan ON a vs OFF +0.43 sigma; fan ON b vs OFF +0.46 sigma; NULL -0.05
  The two rates do not agree, so the 100 kSa/s feature belongs to its shared
  6 s reference, not to the fan.

==============================================================================
G. IS THE FAN VISIBLE BY ANY MEANS? (context for the null)
==============================================================================
  FMCW cfgB, 200 CPIs integrated, moving-target (|v|>0.25 m/s) range profile:
    R= 1.0 m   -45.35 dB
    R= 2.0 m   -42.12 dB
    R= 2.5 m   -42.23 dB
    R= 3.0 m   -42.54 dB
    R= 4.0 m   -42.85 dB
    R= 6.0 m   -43.03 dB
  cfgB PRF=1000 Hz -> unambiguous |v| < 3.11 m/s (500 Hz). A household fan
  blade tip runs 15-30 m/s (2.4-4.8 kHz), so cfgB ALIASES it and cannot
  confirm or deny blade Doppler. CW at 50-100 kSa/s can, so the CW records
  carry the whole argument.

  CW broadband check: median PSD of each band MINUS that record's own
  15-20 kHz reference band, so the session gain offset cancels.
    fanON_50k_a     2.82    1.52    1.01    0.47    0.09
    fanON_50k_b     2.71    1.49    0.93    0.48    0.12
    fanOFF_50k      3.32    1.77    1.14    0.56    0.10
    bands (Hz): 200-600 600-1500 1500-3000 3000-6000 6000-10000

  Quantitative bound on ANY blade micro-Doppler energy, block statistics:
  Every band comes out NEGATIVE (fan-ON lower). That is not a negative fan
  return -- it is a residual session systematic of 0.02-0.5 dB that survives
  the reference-band normalisation. So the honest bound on a fan excess is
  |systematic| + 2*SE, not the one-sided upper limit:
      200-  600 Hz ( 1.2- 3.7 m/s): diff -0.518 dB (-6.0 sig); fan excess < 0.692 dB -> any fan echo >= 7.6 dB BELOW the noise here
      600- 1500 Hz ( 3.7- 9.3 m/s): diff -0.309 dB (-4.8 sig); fan excess < 0.438 dB -> any fan echo >= 9.7 dB BELOW the noise here
     1500- 3000 Hz ( 9.3-18.6 m/s): diff -0.153 dB (-2.9 sig); fan excess < 0.260 dB -> any fan echo >= 12.1 dB BELOW the noise here
     3000- 6000 Hz (18.6-37.3 m/s): diff -0.081 dB (-3.2 sig); fan excess < 0.132 dB -> any fan echo >= 15.1 dB BELOW the noise here
     6000-10000 Hz (37.3-62.1 m/s): diff -0.021 dB (-1.3 sig); fan excess < 0.053 dB -> any fan echo >= 19.1 dB BELOW the noise here
    i.e. 1.2-3.7 3.7-9.3 9.3-18.6 18.6-37.3 37.3-62.1 m/s

  A 40-50 cm household fan at 900-1600 rpm has a blade tip at 19-42 m/s,
  i.e. 3.0-6.7 kHz Doppler. That is precisely the 3000-6000 Hz row above,
  where the bound is tightest and nothing appears.

